<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yanli Li
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of Electrical and Computer Engineering, The University of Sydney, Darlington, 2008, NSW, Australia
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Dong Yuan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of Electrical and Computer Engineering, The University of Sydney, Darlington, 2008, NSW, Australia
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated impressive performance across a wide range of deep learning tasks. Mixture of Experts (MoE) further enhances their capabilities by increasing model width through sparsely activated expert branches, which keeps inference computation efficient. However, the large number of expert weights introduces significant GPU memory pressure, especially in resource-constrained environments such as single-GPU servers. More importantly, MoE inference consists of two fundamentally different stages: a prefill stage where most experts are activated densely, and a decode stage where only a few experts are triggered sparsely. Treating these stages with a uniform scheduling strategy often leads to suboptimal latency and memory usage. To address this, we propose DuoServe-MoE, an inference serving system that explicitly separates prefill and decode stages and applies tailored expert scheduling strategies to each. In the prefill stage, DuoServe-MoE uses a two-stream CUDA pipeline that overlaps expert weight prefetching with the computation of non-MoE layers, limiting expert residency in GPU memory. In the decode stage, a lightweight layer-level predictor trained offline from activation traces is used to prefetch only the most likely activated experts, without requiring any changes to the model. Experiments on 4-bit Mixtral-8×7B and 8×22B models show that DuoServe-MoE improves end-to-end latency by 1.42 to 7.54 times while keeping peak memory usage at only 15 percent of the full model size.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>
Inference Serving System , Mixture of Experts, Resource Constraints, Machine Learning, Edge Computing

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) and their vast parameter scales have achieved remarkable performance in the field of deep learning<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, which have witnessed astonishing accomplishments across different domains and modalities, for example, GPT-4 in ChatGPT<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. To handle complex learning tasks, existing methods typically aim to enhance model capacity by increasing the number of parameters, with Mixture of Experts (MoE) models<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> being a prominent approach. MoEs replace the FeedForward Network (FFN) block in traditional Transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> with multiple expert models that have the same FFN structure, and a gate function is used to select the most suitable expert model for different tokens. Therefore, the number of parameters can be increased by adding more expert models without changing the number of layers. The gate function is employed to achieve sparse activation by selecting the optimal expert models. This sparse activation MoE approach is widely used in large-scale models, such as Switch Transformer, Mixtral, and DeepSeek<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The most significant challenge in delivering LLMs as edge services is memory cost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. Due to the vast number of parameters in LLMs, substantial memory consumption occurs, and the memory capacity of a single GPU is often insufficient for model deployment. The memory cost in LLMs mainly resides in the Mixture of Experts (MoE) layers. For example, When deploying a 4-bit quantized Mixtral-8x7B model onto a single GPU requires 25.1 GB of GPU memory. However, only 2.15 GB is needed for the non-MoE weights, illustrating that the majority of the memory usage comes from the MoE layers. MoESys<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> has employed multi-level caching techniques to mitigate memory cost by caching activated expert models in GPU memory, while non-activated expert models are stored in CPU memory. Huggingface Accelerate<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> chooses to offload the selected expert model weights onto the GPU after the gate function, which introduces significant communication overhead since inference computation is blocked until the expert models are fetched. Therefore, methods such as Pre-gated MoE<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and EdgeMoE<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> attempt to predict the potential expert models before the gate function and prefetch them, aiming to reduce communication overhead. Although prefetching activated experts can speed up inference latency, as the complexity of MoE models increases, predicting which experts will be activated becomes increasingly challenging.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The prediction is based on the MoE activation mechanism. In MoE models, the number of activated experts is determined by the gate function, through a Top-k activation strategy. Switch Transformer used a Top-1 selection, where only one expert out of 32 was activated which is easier to track and predict. In previous MoE inference system designs<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, only Top-1 MoE models were considered. These systems focused on activating a single expert per layer, simplifying the activation prediction and system design. However, this approach may not fully capture the complexity and performance benefits seen in MoE-based LLM models that use Top-2 or higher activation strategies, which are increasingly used to enhance model performance. To more accurately predict model activations, some methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> even choose to fine-tune the LLM, which involves computational costs that bring difficulty in resource-constrained environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the challenges of predicting activated experts, LLMs exhibit different activation patterns for experts during inference. As autoregressive generative models, LLMs handle inference in two phases: prefill and decode. During the prefill stage, multiple tokens in a sequence are processed simultaneously to generate the first token. At the MoE layer, different tokens activate multiple expert models, which is a dense activation. To reduce inference latency, systems such as vLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> and DeepSpeed<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> choose to fetch all expert models onto the GPU, enabling parallel execution of all expert models. However, this approach can lead to Out-of-Memory (OOM) issues when GPU memory is limited. On the other hand, sequentially processing each token and loading only activated experts for each token would significantly increase communication overhead between CPU memory and GPU memory and degrade inference performance. During the decode stage, the LLM only processes one token per iteration, the MoE layer is back to a sparse activation strategy. Therefore, different strategies should be employed for expert scheduling in the two stages of LLM inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address the challenges of expert prediction and the distinct activation patterns between the prefill and decode phases in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) inference, we design an inference serving system called DuoServe-MoE, which applies dedicated expert scheduling strategies for each phase. In summary, our paper presents the following contributions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Considering the two-phase inference characteristics of LLMs, we developed two distinct expert scheduling strategies. In the prefill stage, we employ a two-level pipelining approach to parallelize communication and computation. During the decoding stage, we use a lightweight prediction model to prefetch the experts that will be activated into GPU memory, reducing communication overhead. To the best of our knowledge, we are the first inference serving system to solve the expert fetching problem in the prefill and decode stages.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design a layer-level prediction method to forecast expert selection for each token without changing the model architecture or accuracy. We incorporated the popularity of experts at each layer, the inter-layer experts’ affinity, and the expert activation paths into this learning-based prediction model. Popularity refers to how frequently a particular expert is selected across inference runs, while affinity measures the likelihood of specific experts being activated in consecutive layers. We abstracted the combination of multiple experts per layer into a single expert’s influence on the selection of experts in the subsequent layer, thereby simplifying the training process while maintaining notable prediction accuracy. This lightweight model ensures that all processes of DuoServe-MoE can be completed on the same GPU, alleviating the pressure of resource constraints.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, we propose an inference serving system (DuoServe-MoE) for MoE-based LLMs that reduces GPU memory pressure by offloading expert model weights to CPU memory. We apply DuoServe-MoE to several baselines and conduct experiments on a single GPU. Compared to the baseline, DuoServe-MoE achieves an improvement of 1.42<math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> to 7.54<math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> in end-to-end latency performance. Additionally, DuoServe-MoE’s peak memory usage is only 15% of that required by a GPU-only setup. These results demonstrate DuoServe-MoE’s significant advantages in both latency reduction and memory efficiency, making it a highly effective solution for inference in resource-constrained environments.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background and Motivation</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Mixture of Experts and Activation Mechanism</h3>

<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07379v1/Moe_arch.svg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/131;" width="238" height="131"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Mixture of Experts Architecture in Mixtral.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S2.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07379v1/Popularity.svg" id="S2.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/184;" width="215" height="184"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S2.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">The popularity of experts in each layer.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S2.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07379v1/affinity.svg" id="S2.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/184;" width="215" height="184"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S2.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">The inter-layer affinity between layer 0 and layer 1.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Popularity and Affinity in MoE-based LLMs. Darker colors represent higher probabilities.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mixture of Experts Architecture.</span> Mixture of Experts (MoE)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> is a model architecture originally introduced to tackle the challenges of efficient model parameter utilization and increased representational power. The core idea behind MoE is to partition the workload across multiple specialized sub-models, known as “experts.” The output is expressed as a weighted sum of the outputs from experts. Each expert produces an output based on the input, and the gate function determines the weight assigned to each expert. The gate function values are non-negative and sum to 1, representing the contribution of each expert to the final output. The gate function is typically modeled using a separate neural network. Gshard<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> employs a softmax function to compute the weights based on the input. Therefore, the MoE layer routes the input data to the most relevant experts, combining their outputs to achieve better performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Complexity of MoE Architecture in LLMs.</span> Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.1 Mixture of Experts and Activation Mechanism ‣ 2 Background and Motivation ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates the structure of MoE layers in Mixtral. Unlike the Switch Transformer, Mixtral selects multiple experts to achieve better model accuracy. Mixtral uses a Top-2 mechanism, where each token activates two experts from a pool of eight. While this multi-expert selection enhances model performance, it also leads to challenges for the experts offloading in the inference serving system. In practice, both MoE-Infinity<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> and SiDA-MoE<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> use an LSTM<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> to directly predict the activated experts of all layers at a time, achieving favorable results in networks with a top-1 expert selection mechanism. However, applying this method to scenarios involving multiple expert selections significantly increases the difficulty of prediction, making it less effective in such complex settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Popularity and Affinity in Experts Activation.</span>
Although the activation mechanism of MoE in LLMs is becoming complex, it is not entirely random. Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Mixture of Experts and Activation Mechanism ‣ 2 Background and Motivation ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the popularity of different expert models across different layers in Mixtral, as well as the affinity between different experts in layer 0 and layer 1.
Yao et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> discovered an inter-layer affinity pattern in models employing a Top-1 mechanism: when an expert is activated in one layer, specific experts in the subsequent layer are more likely to be activated. This inter-layer affinity still exists in models using Top-2 or higher expert selection mechanisms. Even with multiple experts being activated per layer, there is a recognizable pattern where certain experts are more likely to be activated in subsequent layers based on previous activations. This demonstrates that certain experts are more frequently activated and that there are discernible relationships between experts in consecutive layers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Challenge #1: Accurately predict the expert selection without changing the LLMs.</span> These expert activation characteristics create opportunities for designing MoE inference serving systems under resource-constrained scenarios. By predicting which experts will be activated at each layer and prefetching activated model weights onto the GPU, memory consumption and data transmission overhead can be reduced to enhance inference speed. Therefore, predicting expert activations accurately becomes a key focus. However, as shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Mixture of Experts and Activation Mechanism ‣ 2 Background and Motivation ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, while there are discernible activation patterns based on popularity and affinity, the distribution is not highly concentrated. Directly designing a heuristic algorithm based solely on these patterns would not achieve high accuracy. Instead, learning-based methods are often preferred as they allow the model to learn activation routes more accurately. However, some prior works fine-tune the LLMs, which affects the model accuracy and raises a challenge for the resource-constrained environment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>MoE in Large Language Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prefill and Decode.</span> In the inference process of LLMs, there are two phases: prefill and decode. During the prefill stage, the model receives the complete input sequence and processes the parallel computation of these tokens. This means that all input tokens can be processed simultaneously during this stage, rather than decoding each token step by step. In the decode stage, the model generates each output token sequentially, using the partially generated output (along with cached features from the prefill stage) to generate new tokens one by one. At this stage, the model uses the already generated tokens and context information to infer the next token, making the process inherently sequential and unsuitable for parallelization like in the prefill stage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Activation Differences in LLMs.</span> For the MoE layer, expert model activation is sparse for a single token. However, the LLM needs to process multiple tokens in parallel, the MoE layer may experience dense activations in the prefill phase. For instance, Mixtral activates all experts during the prefill stage, This creates a situation where the number of activated experts during the prefill stage can be considerably larger compared to the decode stage, leading to significant memory demands and challenges in managing parallel activations efficiently. Unlike the prefill stage, each iteration processes only one token during the decode stage, which means the expert model activation remains sparse.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Challenge #2: Design expert scheduling strategies for prefill and decode stages. </span> Handling the two different activation modes during the inference phase of LLMs presents a significant challenge. In the prefill stage, computing with all experts simultaneously is certainly the fastest option. In inference systems that do not consider memory limitations, such as vLLM, operator fusion is applied in MoE layers, which allows different experts to complete parallel computations within the same operator. However, this approach substantially increases memory consumption, which is unacceptable in resource-constrained scenarios. Thus, minimizing memory usage while reducing latency becomes the core challenge for prefetching scheduling strategies. For the decode stage, although only the activated experts for a single token need to be loaded, the efficiency is highly dependent on the accuracy of the prediction model. If the prediction model makes an error, reloading the correct experts from CPU memory into GPU memory can significantly increase inference latency. Therefore, while efforts should be made to improve prediction model accuracy, a solution is still needed for handling cases where predictions fail. This requires a balance between prefetching efficiency and corrective measures that minimize the latency impact of incorrect predictions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>System Overview</h2>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07379v1/promoe_arch.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:419/280;" width="419" height="280"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>System Overview. The Expert Dispatcher handles expert weight prefetching and scheduling. The State Constructor gathers features for prediction. The Predictor is a trained MLP that forecasts expert activation for the next layer.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address the two issues mentioned earlier, we propose an inference serving system for MoE-based LLMs called DuoServe-MoE. Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 System Overview ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> illustrates the system architecture and workflow of DuoServe-MoE, which is divided into two main stages: offline preprocess and online inference runtime.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the offline preprocess stage, DuoServe-MoE profiles the expert activation trace using a dataset to create a simplified dataset. It captures the activation characteristics of the experts, including their popularity and inter-layer affinity. We use these data as inputs to the predictor, formulating the prediction of the next layer’s activated experts as a classification task for training. Once training is completed, the model is deployed into the inference runtime.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the online phase, users send requests to DuoServe-MoE, which enters the inference runtime. In our scheduling design for the two phases of LLM inference, Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 System Overview ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the blue area representing the prefill stage, while the red area represents the decode stage. Due to the dense activation of expert models during the prefill stage, the LLM serving system directly interacts with the Expert Dispatcher and employs a pipelining strategy to parallelize computation and communication. In the decoding stage, the inference runtime sends the experts index of the activated experts to the State Constructor. The State Constructor integrates the activated experts with the popularity and affinity information collected during the preprocess stage. This consolidated data is then passed to the trained predictor from the preprocessing stage for predicting expert activations in subsequent layers. The Expert Dispatcher uses all of the predictor’s results to deploy the corresponding expert weights onto the GPU before the runtime moves on to the next MoE layer. Once all inference computations are completed, DuoServe-MoE sends the final result back to the user. This two-stage design allows DuoServe-MoE to reduce memory footprint while ensuring minimal inference latency, optimizing both the prefill and decode stages to meet the constraints of resource-limited environments effectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Preprocess</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To effectively solve the problem of predicting multiple expert selections, we designed a learning-based method to train a predictor in the Prepocess stage of DuoServe-MoE. As discussed in Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Mixture of Experts and Activation Mechanism ‣ 2 Background and Motivation ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, fine-tuning a Large Language Model (LLM) for expert prediction poses significant challenges in resource-constrained environments. Additionally, expert predictions depend heavily on inter-affinity and prior expert selections, making iterative predictions particularly detrimental to performance—especially in LLMs with numerous layers. Considering these factors, we propose a layer-level prediction method. Moreover, the data collection, training of the predictor, and final deployment of the LLM can all be accomplished on the same device, thereby reducing additional costs for users and making the solution more accessible for resource-limited scenarios.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Data Collection</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the analysis of Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Mixture of Experts and Activation Mechanism ‣ 2 Background and Motivation ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, the factors of expert selection can be formulated as three: experts’ popularity, inter-layer affinity, and the past expert’s activation path. Therefore, the Preprocess starts from the data collection. Experts Tracer utilizes a small portion of the dataset to record the expert activation paths during the inference process of LLMs. Each inference instance involves the selection of multiple experts from a set of available experts at each layer. We define an “expert activation path” as the sequence of selected experts across all layers during a single inference episode. For each layer <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, the set of selected experts <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is recorded, leading to the following representation of activation paths across <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> layers:</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E=\{E_{l}\mid l\in[1,L],E_{l}\subseteq\{e_{1},e_{2},\dots,e_{M}\}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=\{E_{l}\mid l\in[1,L],E_{l}\subseteq\{e_{1},e_{2},\dots,e_{M}\}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of experts selected at layer <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of available experts. This data forms the basis for constructing both the popularity and affinity matrices, which provide insight into expert selection patterns and inter-layer dependencies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The popularity matrix <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> at layer <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> captures the frequency with which each expert <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is selected. To construct this matrix, we count the number of times each expert is chosen across all inference episodes. The popularity of expert <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> at layer <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is calculated as:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{l}(i)=\frac{\sum_{n=1}^{N}\mathbbm{1}(e_{i}\in E_{l,n})}{\sum_{m=1}^{M}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\mathbbm{1}(e_{m}\in E_{l,n})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{l}(i)=\frac{\sum_{n=1}^{N}\mathbbm{1}(e_{i}\in E_{l,n})}{\sum_{m=1}^{M}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\mathbbm{1}(e_{m}\in E_{l,n})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟙</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is an indicator function that evaluates to 1 if expert <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is selected in the set <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th episode. After counting, the popularity matrix is normalized across each layer to represent the probability distribution of expert selections.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">At last, the affinity matrix <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> captures the relationship between expert selections in consecutive layers. For any expert <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> selected in layer <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, the matrix entry <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{l,l+1}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{l,l+1}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the likelihood of selecting expert <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in layer <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l+1</annotation></semantics></math>, given that <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> was selected in layer <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>. This is computed by examining the joint selection of experts across consecutive layers:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{l,l+1}(i,j)=\frac{\sum_{n=1}^{N}\mathbbm{1}(e_{i}\in E_{l,n}\wedge e_{j}\in E_{l+1,n})}{\sum_{m=1}^{M}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\mathbbm{1}(e_{i}\in E_{l,n}\wedge e_{m}\in E_{l+1,n})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{l,l+1}(i,j)=\frac{\sum_{n=1}^{N}\mathbbm{1}(e_{i}\in E_{l,n}\wedge e_{j}\in E_{l+1,n})}{\sum_{m=1}^{M}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\mathbbm{1}(e_{i}\in E_{l,n}\wedge e_{m}\in E_{l+1,n})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Similar to the popularity matrix, the affinity matrix is normalized so that the sum of probabilities for selecting any expert in layer <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l+1</annotation></semantics></math>, given a selection in layer <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, equals 1. This matrix helps to identify inter-layer expert selection dependencies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Predictor Training Process</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input Construction.</span> The task of predicting expert selections for the next layer is modeled as a supervised learning problem. Given the experts selected from all previous layers, along with the computed popularity and affinity matrices, the goal is to predict the set of experts that will be selected in the next layer. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the set of all selected experts from the previous layers up to layer <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l-1</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{l}=\{E_{0},E_{1},\dots,E_{l-1}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{l}=\{E_{0},E_{1},\dots,E_{l-1}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The input to the model is constructed by combining this set <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, along with the popularity information <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> for layer <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, and the affinity matrix values <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{l-1,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{l-1,l}</annotation></semantics></math> between layers <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{l}=[h_{l},p_{l},a_{l-1,l}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{l}=[h_{l},p_{l},a_{l-1,l}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">To handle varying lengths in <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, we flatten the expert indices and apply zero-padding to a fixed length. The affinity matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{l-1,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{l-1,l}</annotation></semantics></math> and popularity vector <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> are similarly flattened. All components are concatenated into a fixed-length input vector for the MLP. By using the cumulative set of all previous expert selections, the model is able to capture historical selection patterns, which improves its predictive ability for the next layer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Architecture and Training.</span> To predict the expert selections for the next layer, we design a deep seven-layer Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) named ExpertMLP, which takes the constructed state vector <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> as input and outputs a multi-label probability distribution over all experts in the target layer. Compared to traditional shallow MLPs, our model introduces increased representational capacity to capture the nuanced patterns in expert activation conditioned on popularity, inter-layer affinity, and historical selection paths.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The architecture consists of seven fully connected layers with intermediate hidden dimensions progressively reduced from 2048 to 64, followed by a final output layer whose dimension matches the number of experts. Each layer is followed by a Batch Normalization layer and ReLU activation, along with Dropout (with a default rate of 0.1) applied after each hidden layer to prevent overfitting. This design balances depth and regularization, enabling the model to generalize well across varying expert selection distributions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Because DuoServe-MoE needs to predict multiple activated experts, which can be considered as multiple labels. Therefore, the model is trained using a Binary Cross-Entropy loss function<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, which compares the predicted probabilities of the experts with the ground truth expert selections. The loss function is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle L(\theta)=-\sum_{n=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L(\theta)=-\sum_{n=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\left[y_{n}\log(\sigma(f_{\theta}(s_{n})))\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left[y_{n}\log(\sigma(f_{\theta}(s_{n})))\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\left.+(1-y_{n})\log(1-\sigma(f_{\theta}(s_{n})))\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left.+(1-y_{n})\log(1-\sigma(f_{\theta}(s_{n})))\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the one-hot encoded ground truth expert selection for the <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th sample, and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the sigmoid function used to convert the model’s outputs to probabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This training process allows the model to learn the expert selection patterns using the previously computed popularity and affinity matrix, leveraging the relationships across multiple layers to improve predictive accuracy.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Inference Runtime</h2>

<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07379v1/prefill.svg" id="S5.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/218;" width="381" height="218"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F4.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Prefill</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07379v1/decode.svg" id="S5.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/217;" width="405" height="217"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F4.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Decode</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Prefill and Decode scheduling mechanism in DuoServe-MoE. The top part of each figure shows the memory view, and the bottom part shows the CUDA streams view.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Inference Runtime Workflow</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At the start of the inference runtime, DuoServe-MoE fetches the model weights onto the GPU. In MoE-based LLMs, the non-MoE model weights account for only about 10% of the total weights, so DuoServe-MoE chooses to fetch them all onto the GPU to maintain sufficient inference performance. For the MoE layer weights, DuoServe-MoE establishes an expert cache on the GPU. Weights for activated experts are prefetched from the CPU expert cache to the GPU expert cache just before expert execution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To minimize memory consumption, the GPU expert cache is provisioned to match the number of experts activated per layer during inference. For MoE models with a Top-k selection strategy, DuoServe-MoE sets the GPU cache size to k, ensuring that exactly the required number of expert weights are prefetched and resident in memory without redundancy. For instance, in the Mixtral 8×7B model where Top-2 is used, the GPU expert cache size is set to 2. This design avoids unnecessary memory overhead while enabling concurrent prefetch and compute through our dual-stream scheduling mechanism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Once the serving system has deployed the model, users send requests to the inference runtime, and the Expert Dispatcher manages the prefetching and scheduling of the experts. Due to the different activation modes of expert models in the prefill and decode stages, we employ two distinct scheduling approaches. Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5 Inference Runtime ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates DuoServe-MoE’s mechanism for handling expert layers from the perspectives of memory scheduling and CUDA streams. In the prefill stage, given the large number of tokens and all experts being activated, there is no need for predictions from the predictor. We use the Mixtral model as a demonstration case, where each layer has 8 experts, and 2 of them are selected for activation. Therefore, the CPU expert cache size is 8 experts per layer, while the GPU expert cache size is 2. If DuoServe-MoE serves other LLMs, the size of the expert cache will be according to the model architecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the Expert Dispatcher, we utilize a multi-CUDA stream<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> pipeline technique, allowing weight communication and operator computation to be executed concurrently, which can minimize latency and keep memory consumption low. In the decode stage, we leverage the predictor trained during the preprocess phase to predict future experts, thereby reducing additional communication overhead.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Prefill Stage</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 System Overview ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the LLM serving system directly manages scheduling through the Expert Dispatcher during the prefill stage. Since all experts need to be processed, we focus on how to fetch all the experts of each layer into the GPU expert cache and compute them efficiently.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We establish two CUDA streams: one as the computation stream and the other as the communication stream. DuoServe-MoE uses the communication stream to prefetch the first expert weights into the GPU expert cache, while the computation stream concurrently performs computation for the non-MoE layers. Before entering the MoE layer, we synchronize the two streams to ensure that the expert weights are loaded into the GPU expert cache. This approach minimizes latency by overlapping communication and computation, thus maximizing the leverage of GPU resources during the prefill stage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">After the gate function in the MoE layer calculates the expert selection for all tokens, the LLM serving system groups the tokens by their assigned experts, ensuring that each expert performs a batch-processing operation for all associated tokens. Therefore, each expert weight only needs to be fetched once, optimizing memory operations. The system enters a two-stage pipelined phase. The communication stream starts fetching the next expert from the CPU expert cache once the first expert in the GPU expert cache has completed the computation. Due to the high parallelism of GPU, the operator in the computation stream is completed fast. However, constrained by the limited PCIe bandwidth, fetching expert weights in the communication stream is slower compared to the expert operator computation. As shown in the stream view in Figure <a href="#S5.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 5 Inference Runtime ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a>, we set synchronization points after each expert is fetched to ensure that no incorrect expert weights are used for computation. In the memory view of Figure <a href="#S5.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 5 Inference Runtime ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a>, the two experts in the GPU expert cache are always in a state where one is being used for computation and the other is being fetched. This ensures that the communication stream is constantly fetching new weights without having to wait for the computation stream, thereby minimizing GPU memory usage while ensuring low latency. This balance between prefetching and computation effectively optimizes resource utilization during the prefill phase. Once all experts have completed their computations, the LLM serving system unpacks the results and finalizes the computation for each individual token. After all layers have completed their computations, the LLM generates the first token and proceeds to the decoding stage.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Decode Stage</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the inference phase of an LLM, multiple iterations of computation are required to complete a request. Only the first round is the prefill stage, while all subsequent rounds are in the decoding stage, making optimization of the decoding stage crucial. Unlike the prefill stage, the decoding stage only processes one token at a time. This means that only 2 experts per layer need to be loaded. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 System Overview ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, during the decoding stage, we utilize the prediction model trained in the preprocessing phase to predict the expert selection for the current token.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the first layer, the Expert Dispatcher fetches the expert models into the GPU after the gate function completes the selection. Simultaneously, the LLM serving system sends the first layer’s expert selection to the State Constructor for storage. The State Constructor retrieves the corresponding popularity vector for the upcoming layer and combines it with the inter-layer affinity vector from the previous layer’s expert selection, using this data as input for the predictor. Since the predictor itself is also a neural network, which involves the computation on GPU, we add an additional prediction stream on top of the prefill stage to achieve parallel processing and minimize the extra overhead of prediction. As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 5 Inference Runtime ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a>, when Layer N begins the expert computation, the predictor starts predicting the next layer’s experts. After the first expert in the GPU expert cache completes its computation, the communication stream immediately starts prefetching the experts for the next layer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To prevent race conditions during parallel operations, DuoServe-MoE has two synchronization points during the decoding stage. The first synchronization point ensures that prefetching has finished before Expert 1 begins computation, given that prefetching latency is higher than the operator computation in the computation stream. Additionally, the experts selected by the gate function are compared with those loaded in the GPU expert cache. If there is a mismatch, the correct experts are re-fetched from the CPU expert cache to ensure that the expert weights used are correct. The second synchronization point occurs after the first expert completes its computation and after the predictor in the prediction stream has predicted the next layer’s expert selection, allowing prefetching for the new layer to begin. After each round of computation, the State Constructor clears the stored activation trace and incorporates the new expert selections for the next round, continuing this process until the request is fully processed.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Evaluation</h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation.</span> To implement DuoServe-MoE, we utilized the state-of-the-art LLM inference framework vLLM and employed CUDA pinned memory<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> to enable efficient CPU offloading. Additionally, we leveraged CUDA streams to implement a multi-stream pipeline design, enhancing parallelism and improving system efficiency. For the prediction model, we used PyTorch for training and deployed it on the GPU to minimize latency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardware.</span> Since DuoServe-MoE’s performance is influenced by different scenarios, we evaluated it under two distinct settings in our experiments. The first scenario involves a GPU-based edge server, which consists of an AMD Threadripper PRO 3945WX 12-Core CPU, 128GB RAM, and an NVIDIA RTX A5000 GPU with 24 GB GPU memory, running on Ubuntu 20.04 OS. The second scenario involves using a more powerful GPU-based edge server, specifically an AMD Ryzen Threadripper PRO 5955WX 16-Core CPU, 128GB RAM, and an NVIDIA RTX A6000 GPU with 48 GB GPU memory, running on Ubuntu 24.04 OS. Both of them use PCIe4.0 with 16GT/s bandwidth. This scenario allows us to evaluate DuoServe-MoE’s scalability across different computational resources by comparing its performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models.</span> We evaluated DuoServe-MoE using two different sizes of the Mixtral model, which are decode-only architecture Large Language Models. First, both models were quantized to 4-bit precision using the AWQ<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, and the inference was performed using vLLM’s quantization operators. We download the pre-trained model weights from Huggingface. Table <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the structure of the two models, providing a comparative overview of their configurations. Both models have 8 experts in each layer and employ a top-2 selection mechanism, with a temperature-based sampling process.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>The Major Configurations of the Backbone Models.</figcaption>
<div id="S6.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:241.5pt;height:39.2pt;vertical-align:-17.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(6.1pt,-1.0pt) scale(1.05328981804037,1.05328981804037) ;">
<table id="S6.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S6.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">Model</span></th>
<th id="S6.T1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T1.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">Layers</span></th>
<th id="S6.T1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T1.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">Active Para.</span></th>
<th id="S6.T1.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T1.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">Total Para.</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S6.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">Mixtral-8x7B</span></th>
<th id="S6.T1.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T1.2.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">32</span></th>
<td id="S6.T1.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T1.2.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">12.9B</span></td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T1.2.1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">46.7B</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b"><span id="S6.T1.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">Mixtral-8x22B</span></th>
<th id="S6.T1.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b"><span id="S6.T1.2.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">56</span></th>
<td id="S6.T1.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b"><span id="S6.T1.2.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">39B</span></td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b"><span id="S6.T1.2.1.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">141B</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<figure id="S6.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07379v1/bar_plot.svg" id="S6.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/255;" width="476" height="255"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>The performance evaluation across different test sets. The red bar represents the Time to First Token (TTFT), and the blue bar represents the End to End latency.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Workload.</span> In our experiments, we evaluated DuoServe-MoE using only single-request scenarios. This setting aligns with real-world usage in resource-constrained environments, where LLMs are typically deployed on edge or single-GPU servers to serve one user at a time. More importantly, batch processing is incompatible with the sparse expert activation pattern of the decode phase. When multiple requests are processed in parallel, the overall expert activation becomes significantly denser, which dilutes the sparsity benefits that DuoServe-MoE is designed to exploit during decoding. As a result, the predictor-driven expert prefetching mechanism would become less effective, and memory usage would increase sharply. Additionally, due to limited computational resources, we rely on CPU offloading to reduce GPU memory pressure. Introducing batching under these conditions would amplify communication overhead and further increase inference latency, which could severely degrade the quality of service. For these reasons, we focus on single-request evaluation to faithfully reflect DuoServe-MoE’s intended deployment scenarios and optimization assumptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We selected two different datasets to evaluate DuoServe-MoE. One is the question-answering dataset, SQuAD<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, and the other is a question-answering dataset focused on math problems, Orca-Math<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. By using different tasks, we aim to assess the scalability of the predictor and understand the expert selection preferences of MoE for different types of tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span> For performance evaluation, we used common LLM serving metrics, including average end-to-end latency, time to first token (TTFT), and the number of output tokens per second (throughput). Additionally, we analyzed peak memory usage and the hit rate of the prediction model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
To analyze DuoServe-MoE’s(<span id="S6.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DuoServe</span>) performance, we compared it against two state-of-the-art MoE-based LLM inference serving systems. Both systems use a strategy of offloading non-activated weights to CPU memory to reduce GPU memory usage. The first system is Huggingface’s Accelerate framework (<span id="S6.SS1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acc</span>)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, which loads activated experts onto the GPU after selection is made by the gate function. The second system is MoESys (<span id="S6.SS1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MoESys</span>)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, which prefetches all experts for each layer into GPU memory to reduce the communication waiting overhead.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Since various factors can influence LLMs inference such as the implementation of attention mechanisms and the optimization of key-value (KV) caching, we replicated these two baselines within the vLLM framework to ensure consistency in all factors except the MoE scheduling mechanism. Other methods, such as Pre-gated MoE and Sida MoE, require fine-tuning the LLMs, making them impractical for this scenario. As a result, these methods were not included as baselines for comparison.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Performance Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Latency Metrics.</span> To analyze DuoServe-MoE’s latency metrics, we tested it using two different models and two different devices, using unseen data from two different datasets. The average TTFT and end-to-end latency results are presented in Figure <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 6.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Overall, DuoServe-MoE achieved an improvement of 1.78<math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> to 3.8<math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> in TTFT compared to the baselines, while the end-to-end latency improved by 1.42<math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> to 7.54<math id="S6.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>. Compared to ACC and DuoServe-MoE, MoESys exhibits much higher latency, primarily because MoESys requires transferring a larger size of expert models, leading to additional communication overhead. In MoE-based LLMs, the expert models tend to be quite large, and the GPU prefetching cost is significantly higher compared to regular MoE models. Therefore, MoESys experiences a communication overhead that far exceeds the computational cost, which results in weaker performance for MoESys in LLM inference. In contrast to ACC, DuoServe-MoE benefits from the prediction model, allowing expert model prefetching, which enables overlapping of the computation and communication processes. This capability improves performance in the decode stage. During the prefill stage, DuoServe-MoE performs computation on one expert while prefetching another, thereby reducing latency through parallelism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When comparing datasets, DuoServe-MoE performs better on Orca, achieving a 1.52<math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> improvement in end-to-end latency compared to ACC, whereas in SQuAD, the improvement is only 1.47<math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>. This demonstrates that MoE models exhibit different expert selection tendencies across various tasks. DuoServe-MoE’s predictor has a slightly higher error rate on the SQuAD dataset, which results in some experts needing to be reloaded after the gate function makes its selection, increasing communication overhead. A more detailed analysis of the prediction model can be found in section <a href="#S6.SS4" title="6.4 Predictor Overhead Discussion ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>. In the model comparison, DuoServe-MoE performs slightly better on the 7B model compared to the 22B model. This is because the expert models in the 22B model are larger, which increases communication overhead when the predictor makes an error, thereby affecting the end-to-end results. In the comparison across different machines, DuoServe-MoE demonstrates more significant performance improvements on the A6000 due to its higher computational power. Overall, DuoServe-MoE employs a heuristic pipelining approach during the prefill stage, resulting in more stable performance. In the decoding stage, DuoServe-MoE uses a learning-based method, which introduces slight variability in performance but still maintains sufficient scalability for practical use.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07379v1/cdf_plot.svg" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:333/272;" width="333" height="272"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>The Cumulative Distribution Function (CDF) of decoding throughput. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Throughput Metrics.</span> Since end-to-end latency is influenced by the number of decode iterations, making it challenging to directly compare performance differences during the decode stage, we use throughput (tokens per second) as a metric for comparison. Figure <a href="#S6.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 6.2 Performance Evaluation ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> presents the Cumulative Distribution Function (CDF) of throughput for DuoServe-MoE and the two baseline systems. Compared to the baselines, DuoServe-MoE achieves performance improvements of 1.78<math id="S6.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> to 7.04<math id="S6.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>, with more significant gains observed on machines with lower computational power.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This greater improvement on lower-powered machines is due to the identical PCIe bandwidth of both machines, resulting in similar communication overhead. However, the weaker machine incurs slightly higher computational costs, which amplifies DuoServe-MoE’s performance advantage. In the cumulative distribution comparison, DuoServe-MoE’s distribution is broader compared to the baselines, which can be attributed to errors from the prediction model. Despite this, DuoServe-MoE’s overall throughput is superior to the other baselines, indicating that the cost associated with prediction model errors is acceptable in practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Memory Consumption Analysis</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further evaluate the memory optimization of the system, we tested the peak memory usage. Since different devices do not impact memory usage, and different datasets only cause minor variations due to differences in KV cache size, Table <a href="#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 6.3 Memory Consumption Analysis ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents peak memory usage statistics only for different models. In addition to the baselines and DuoServe-MoE, we also included a “GPU Only” scenario in Table <a href="#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 6.3 Memory Consumption Analysis ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for reference. “GPU Only” refers to loading all model weights onto the GPU to ensure optimal performance, but the resulting memory cost is prohibitive. As shown in Table <a href="#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 6.3 Memory Consumption Analysis ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, a Mixtral 8x22B model requires at least 138 GB of GPU memory for its weights, which is clearly infeasible for any resource-constrained environment. Therefore, we did not include the GPU-only scenario as a regular baseline for performance comparison, as it would lead to out-of-memory (OOM) situations, making it impossible for the system to function properly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the comparison between DuoServe-MoE and the baselines, DuoServe-MoE’s memory usage is better than MoESys but slightly higher than ACC. This is because MoESys deploys all expert weights for each layer onto the GPU, leading to additional memory overhead. Both ACC and DuoServe-MoE only fetch the activated experts to the GPU, but DuoServe-MoE also includes a prediction model, which introduces some extra memory usage. However, compared to this minor increase in memory usage, the performance gains brought by the prediction model are much more significant, making this additional memory cost acceptable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, although MoE models maintain computational requirements similar to dense models due to their sparse activation mechanism, the difference in memory usage is significant. In LLMs, because the decode stage processes only one token at a time, the inference bottleneck is still largely determined by memory usage. In MoE-based LLM inference serving, finding a balance between memory consumption and latency is of utmost importance. DuoServe-MoE addresses this balance by using intelligent expert prediction and optimized scheduling, ultimately making MoE models more accessible and practical for deployment.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>The Peak Memory Consumption in different models. GPU only represents deploying the entire model on GPU.</figcaption>
