</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4 </span>Predictor Overhead Discussion</h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In DuoServe-MoE, the additional overhead of the predictor mainly involves two aspects: training and inference. To ensure that DuoServe-MoE can be fully deployed in resource-constrained environments, it is necessary that all stages are executable on the same device, which is why we opted for a lightweight model for the predictor. During the data collection phase (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Predictor Training Process ‣ 4 Preprocess ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>), we select corresponding datasets based on different tasks and employ a scheduling approach similar to ACC to ensure the efficiency of the collection process. DuoServe-MoE uses only 2.5% of the dataset for training. The entire data collection process takes only 5 to 8 hours, depending on the computational power of the device and the size of the model. For training, since the predictor uses an MLP structure, only 15 to 20 epochs are needed for Mixtral 8x7B, while Mixtral 8x22B requires just 40 to 45 epochs. As a result, the entire preprocessing process takes no more than 9 hours, which is completely acceptable compared to fine-tuning an LLM. This approach significantly reduces computational demands, making DuoServe-MoE feasible for deployment in resource-limited scenarios without compromising efficiency. Since the data requirements are minimal and the model itself is not altered, users can also deploy the preprocessing process during actual inference. This allows for data collection and inference serving to occur simultaneously, thereby improving efficiency and reducing the need for a separate preprocess phase.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>The hit rate of the predictor with different models and datasets.</figcaption>
<div id="S6.T3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:241.5pt;height:68.6pt;vertical-align:-31.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(11.6pt,-3.3pt) scale(1.10599919329276,1.10599919329276) ;">
<table id="S6.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
