<td id="S6.T3.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S6.T3.2.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">60.23%</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S6.T3.2.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">92.31%</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="S6.T3.2.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">Mixtral8x22B</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T3.2.1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">Orca</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T3.2.1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">56.21%</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T3.2.1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">90.45%</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b"><span id="S6.T3.2.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">Squad</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b"><span id="S6.T3.2.1.5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">54.16%</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b"><span id="S6.T3.2.1.5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">90.31%</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite using a very small dataset and a lightweight model, DuoServe-MoE still maintains a high prediction accuracy. Table <a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 6.4 Predictor Overhead Discussion ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Cache Scheduling for Efficient MoE LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the predictor’s accuracy across different datasets and models. The Top-2 metric indicates the accuracy of both experts being correctly predicted (“Top-2 hit”) and the accuracy of at least one expert being correctly predicted (“at least one hit”). For the Top-2 metric, larger models perform slightly worse than smaller models. This is due to the instability of outputs in larger models, where even with identical prompts, responses can be different. This indicates that MoE selection in larger models is more diverse. Across different datasets, the predictor’s hit rate shows minimal differences, suggesting that the model has effectively learned the gate function’s selection mechanism. Overall, the predictor’s “at least one hit” rate exceeds 90%, ensuring that DuoServe-MoE does not incur significant additional communication overhead, thereby maintaining efficient inference performance. In the inference runtime, the predictor requires only around 300 MB of GPU memory and adds approximately 0.6 ms of latency. Additionally, DuoServe-MoE uses CUDA streams to overlap this latency through parallelization, effectively minimizing the impact. As a result, the extra overhead introduced by the predictor is entirely acceptable.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficient LLMs inference serving.</span> With the rapid development of Large Language Models, efficient inference serving has become a key area of research. Some prior work focuses on optimizing models through compression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> techniques, such as model pruning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, knowledge distillation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, and quantization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. These methods can be combined with DuoServe-MoE, which uses AWQ quantization for model deployment. Additionally, some methods address the bottleneck of insufficient GPU memory by using CPU offloading<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> and optimizing the KV cache <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. These methods are better suited for dense models. DuoServe-MoE also uses CPU offloading but is designed specifically with GPU efficiency in mind.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Optimization Systems for MoE model.</span> For inference serving of MoE-based LLMs, the research can be broadly divided into two categories. The first involves distributed inference across multiple GPUs<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, focusing on parallelizing expert computations and using specific expert allocation methods to reduce communication overhead, which is a major challenge in distributed scenarios. The second category targets resource-constrained scenarios involving a single GPU. These approaches<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> generally adopt CPU offloading as the main scheduling principle, combined with predictions of which experts to activate. However, some methods<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> require fine-tuning of LLMs, which is impractical on a single GPU. Due to differences in the specific use cases of these methods or the difficulty of reproducing certain learning-based approaches, we don’t select them for comparison in our experimental baselines.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper presents DuoServe-MoE, an inference serving system designed to address two key challenges in deploying MoE-based LLMs in resource-constrained environments. DuoServe-MoE employs two different expert scheduling mechanisms to handle the distinct expert activation patterns in the prefill and decode stages. Additionally, DuoServe-MoE leverages a learning-based predictor to prefetch activated experts, thereby minimizing additional memory overhead. Experimental results show that DuoServe-MoE achieves an improvement of 1.42<math id="S8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> to 7.54<math id="S8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> in end-to-end latency, demonstrating its effectiveness in optimizing inference performance for MoE-based LLMs under resource limitations.</p>
</div>
</section>
